# d3Sea — dialogue draft
#
#
#   node tools/dialogue/compile.mjs tools/dialogue/drafts/d3Sea.dlg --check
#   node tools/dialogue/compile.mjs tools/dialogue/drafts/d3Sea.dlg
#
# ── SYMBOLS ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
#   =  a node. What the NPC says when this point is reached. The name after
#      it is only a label for you; it is not used by the game.
#
#   *  an option. The text is what appears ON THE BUTTON.
#
#   >  what the PLAYER says after choosing that option.
#
#   <  what the NPC says back.
#
#   %  the Irish for the line directly above it. A bare % is a placeholder
#      for Irish not yet written, and is ignored. English alone is a
#      perfectly valid draft — that is the point of working this way.
#
#   @  a directive. See below.
#
#   #  a comment. Ignored.
#
# Every > < = * line may be repeated to make several lines on screen. Each
# one gets its own % beneath it if it has Irish. Irish and English are paired
# BY POSITION, so a missing or extra line silently shifts every translation
# after it out of step — which reads as a mistranslation rather than a
# formatting slip. The compiler refuses to build if the counts disagree.
#
# ── DIRECTIVES ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
#   @file NAME          which data file to write into  (required, once)
#   @encounter N        index into fixedEncounters     (default 0)
#
#   @when quest X       only if quest X is running
#   @when !quest X      only if quest X has not started
#   @when done X        only if quest X is finished
#   @when note X        only if the player has note X
#   @when !note X       only if they do not
#
#   @note X             set note X
#   @quest X            start quest X
#   @done X             finish quest X
#   @hold               keep the card open rather than closing after this
#   @first              (options) offer this only once
#   @exit               (options) this one ends the conversation
#   @silent             (options) no reply card
#   @again EN / GA      the nudge line when the player lingers
#
# A node with options MUST have one marked @exit: cards with buttons cannot
# be dismissed by swiping, so without it the player is trapped.
#
# ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────


@file       d3Sea
@encounter  0

= node 0
@when !quest q_baile
@note met_muireann
@again Well? Speak. / Bhuel? Labhair.
May the stone of the shore greet you, man of the sea.
% 
May the current that guided you here honour you.
% 

* May the wind greet you
% 
@note gave_greeting
> May the wind that carried me greet you.
% 
> May the sea that bore me greet you.
% 
< What the sea has brought us,
% 
< let the land receive well.
% 
< What the sea has carried,
% 
< the land may trust.
% 

* Who are you?
% 
> Who are you, woman of the stones,
% 
> who speaks to me so?
% 
< One who is here.
% 
< One who watches.
% 
< That is all the answer you get yet, and it is enough.
% 

* Farewell
% 
@exit
@silent

= node 1
@again Well? From where? / Bhuel? Cad as?
From where were you blown to us?
% 
It is a long road for your little boat
% 
from wherever it was built, I think.
% 

* From Skye
% 
@note answered_plain
> From the grey shore of Skye I have come.
% 
> Long rowing,
% 
> and the sea rising every day of it.
% 
< That is no small thing.
% 
< Your back is telling you so, I expect,
% 
< and it will not stop telling you tonight or tomorrow.
% 

* From where the hound lost the scent
% 
@note answered_careful
> From where the hound lost the scent
% 
> and the hawk lost the wind beneath her wing.
% 
> From where the fire was that is not now burning.
% 
< So, so.
% 
< That is a careful answer.
% 
< You would not be so careful were there no reason for it.
% 

* Farewell
% 
@exit
@silent

= node 2
@when !note knows_boyne
Attend to me now.
% 
You have beached your little boat
% 
at the mouth of Bóinn the bright and winding,
% 
and this shore belongs to her before anyone.
% 

* Whose shore is it, then?
% 
> Whose shore is it I have been blown upon, then?
% 
< It belongs first and last to Bóinn.
% 
< Do not forget that while you are drinking from the river.
% 

* Farewell
% 
@exit
@silent

= node 3
@note knows_boyne
@quest q_baile
@hold
@again Well? Do you want more? / Bhuel? An bhfuil tuilleadh uait?
As for the chieftain:
% 
you have put your prow upon the shore of Fionnbarra mac Dubhloingse,
% 
chieftain of this grey headland
% 
and of the three valleys that run from it like fingers from a hand.
% 

* What kind of man is he?
% 
@first
> What kind of man is this Fionnbarra?
% 
< A hand often raised in anger,
% 
< and raised as often in welcome.
% 
< A man of temper and of patience in the one body,
% 
< and his hounds are many.
% 

* Is he a just man?
% 
@first
> Is he a just man,
% 
> this man whose land I am walking?
% 
< He is not unjust,
% 
< and that is more than I would say for every one of his line.
% 
< His grandfather's grandfather gave oaths to the Tuatha
% 
< that were not entirely kept,
% 
< and they gave in return a curse upon his cattle
% 
< that was not entirely lifted.
% 
< The milk of this place has tasted of broken promises ever since --
% 
< or of the wild garlic in the lower pastures, depending who you ask.
% 

* Where is the ringfort?
% 
@note knows_road_west
> Where is the ringfort from here? The day is going.
% 
< Half a morning's walk northwest along the track.
% 
< You will not miss it.
% 
< You will find a fire there, and a full bowl, and dry ground.
% 

* Who are you?
% 
@note knows_muireann
@first
> And who are you yourself, woman of the rock,
% 
> watching the water?
% 
< Muireann.
% 
< I keep watch on the mouth of the river, as my mother did before me.
% 
< Tell Mór at the ringfort it was I who sent you.
% 

* Farewell
% 
@exit
@silent

= node 4
@hold
@again Well? / Bhuel?
The toll-man will meet you at the river ford,
% 
for the ravens have already carried word of your coming,
% 
and he is a man who rises early to meet opportunity.
% 

* What will I say to him?
% 
@note has_druid_word
@first
> And what will I say to that man
% 
> when he stands in front of me?
% 
< Say to him that the Druid of the Cliff-Mouth knows your name.
% 
< You need say no more than that.
% 
< And say no more.
% 

* What kind of man is he?
% 
@first
> What kind of man is this toll-man?
% 
< He has a face like a stone that has been disappointed.
% 
< He is not a loveable man and does not wish to be.
% 
< A chieftain needs such a man all the same.
% 

* Farewell
% 
@exit
@silent

= node 5
@when note answered_careful
@hold
@again Well? / Bhuel?
One more thing, since you are a careful sort.
% 
Fionnbarra has a brother,
% 
and I will not shout his name from a clifftop
% 
as a fool shouts the name of a wolf into a dark wood.
% 

* A brother?
% 
@note knows_cathan
@first
> A brother? What name is on him, and what is his portion?
% 
< Cathán.
% 
< He holds the southern wood, and three fishing-rights on the tributary,
% 
< and an opinion of himself that would shame the sun for brightness.
% 

* And if I meet him?
% 
@first
> If I meet him at a ford or a gate or a fire,
% 
> what should I do?
% 
< Do not look at what is shown to you.
% 
< That which speaks may not be him,
% 
< and that which smiles is not his smile.
% 
< Know the man by what he will not do.
% 

* What is the danger?
% 
@first
> What danger is in it for me, and I going west?
% 
< Thin enough.
% 
< As thin as first ice on the stream,
% 
< as thin as smoke on a summer wind.
% 
< Go your road and miss his road.
% 

* Farewell
% 
@exit
@silent

= node 6
@when quest q_baile
@again Away west with you. / Siar leat.
May the Boyne remember your name to the sea.
% 
May the sea speak your name to the deep places.
% 
May the deep places hold you,
% 
neither too long nor too short.
% 

= node 7
@when done q_baile
@hold
@again Well? / Bhuel?
So you saw the ringfort.
% 
Come back to me if you want news.
% 
